# ==============================================================================
# eBPF SERVICE MESH SIMULATION SUITE
# ==============================================================================
# Architecture Note:
# Gateway Services (Service 1 & 2) do not implement explicit route handlers. 
# They act as transparent reverse proxies with catch-all "/" routing handlers.
# When they receive a request on ANY path (e.g. /api/work or /health):
#   1. They forward it to the Stable Backend (127.0.0.1:8083) on the same path.
#   2. During this forwarding, the socket connect() syscall is intercepted by eBPF.
#   3. eBPF then applies Cgroup firewall blocks (for S2) or Canary port rewrites (for S1).

###
### TEST 1: Gateway Allowed (Service 1) -> /api/work
# Path /api/work is forwarded to 8083. eBPF will allow the connection and
# probabilistically rewrite the target port to 8084 (Canary v2) 50% of the time.
GET http://localhost:8081/api/work

###
### TEST 2: Gateway Blocked (Service 2) -> /api/work
# Path /api/work is forwarded to 8083. eBPF connect hook identifies Service 2's
# PID on the blocklist and drops the connection, returning "Operation not permitted".
GET http://localhost:8082/api/work

###
### TEST 3: Direct Call to Stable Backend v1 (Service 3)
GET http://localhost:8083/health

###
### TEST 4: Direct Call to Canary Backend v2 (Service 4)
GET http://localhost:8084/health
